---
import SimLayout from '../../../layouts/SimLayout.astro';
---

<SimLayout
  areaSlug="electronics"
  title="Resistor"
  description="Nothing in here obeys Ohm's law. Electrons are pulled steadily by the field and then knocked back to random by the lattice, over and over, and the average of that stop-start crawl is a speed proportional to the push. That average is the current, the constant of proportionality is the resistance, and every scrap of energy the field gives the electrons is handed to the lattice as heat."
>
  <div class="sim">
    <div class="sim-stage">
      <canvas id="res" width="1000" height="700"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="sim-panel">
      <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <button class="btn" id="run">Pause</button>
        <button class="btn ghost" id="reset">Reset</button>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Plot below the scene</label>
        <div class="seg" id="plot">
          <button data-p="scope" class="active">Velocity</button>
          <button data-p="iv">I–V</button>
          <button data-p="power">Heat</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Material</label>
        <div class="seg" id="mat">
          <button data-m="nichrome" class="active">Nichrome</button>
          <button data-m="copper">Copper</button>
          <button data-m="tungsten">Tungsten</button>
          <button data-m="carbon">Carbon</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Voltage across it <b id="vval">1.00 V</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="v" min="0" max="600" step="1" value="100" />
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Length L <b id="lval">—</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="len" min="5" max="200" step="1" value="50" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>short, easy</span><span>long, hard</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Cross-section A <b id="aval">—</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="area" min="1" max="200" step="1" value="10" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>thin, hard</span><span>fat, easy</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Ambient temperature <b id="tval">20 °C</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="tamb" min="-50" max="200" step="1" value="20" />
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Show</label>
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="thermal" checked /> Thermal motion</label>
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="heat" /> Let it heat itself up</label>
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="trail" checked /> Follow one electron</label>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Time rate <b id="rateval">1.0×</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="rate" min="10" max="300" step="5" value="100" />
      </div>

      <div class="readout">
        <div class="row"><span id="l1">—</span><b id="r1">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l2">—</span><b id="r2">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l3">—</span><b id="r3">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l4">—</span><b id="r4">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l5">—</span><b id="r5">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l6">—</span><b id="r6">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l7">—</span><b id="r7">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l8">—</span><b id="r8">—</b></div>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-faint);margin:0;line-height:1.5">
        The picture is slowed down and the drift is drawn far larger than life.
        The numbers in the readout are the real ones for the metal you picked.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="observe">
    <li>
      Watch a single electron with <b>Follow one electron</b> on. It does not
      sail down the wire. It rattles, gets grabbed by the field for a moment,
      slams into the lattice, forgets which way it was going, and starts over.
      The current is not the speed of that rattling, it is the tiny
      <b>net crawl</b> the rattling has on top of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Turn <b>thermal motion</b> off. Now the drift is all that is left and it
      is obvious. Turn it back on and it vanishes into the noise, which is
      honest: the thermal speed is around a million times the drift speed. Both
      numbers are in the readout, and the ratio is the reason a wire carrying
      amps still looks like nothing is happening.
    </li>
    <li>
      The graph of average velocity settles onto a flat line. That is the whole
      of Ohm's law: the field <b>accelerates</b>, so without collisions the
      speed would grow forever, but a collision every τ seconds keeps resetting
      it. The average lands at <b>aτ</b>, proportional to the field, and current
      proportional to voltage is nothing but that.
    </li>
    <li>
      Make the sample twice as long. The same voltage is now spread over twice
      the distance, so the field is halved, so the drift is halved, so
      <b>R doubles</b>. Now make it twice as fat instead: same field, same drift,
      but twice as many lanes carrying it, so <b>R halves</b>. That is the whole
      content of R = ρL/A, and neither half of it needed an experiment.
    </li>
    <li>
      Raise the temperature. The lattice shakes harder, an electron gets less
      far before hitting something, τ falls and <b>resistance rises</b>. Carbon
      does the opposite, because heat there frees more carriers than it
      scatters, and that sign difference is why carbon and metal resistors age
      and drift in opposite directions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every collision hands the field's energy to the lattice, which is where
      <b>P = VI</b> physically goes. Switch on <b>let it heat itself up</b> with
      tungsten and turn the voltage to full. The metal glows, its resistance
      climbs as it does, and on the <b>I–V plot</b> the line <b>bends over</b>:
      a light bulb is not an ohmic device, it just contains one when it is cold.
    </li>
    <li>
      Compare copper with nichrome at the same size. Copper's resistivity is
      about sixty times lower, so at a few volts it draws an absurd current and
      cooks itself. That is why one of them is sold as wire and the other as a
      heating element: <b>same physics, different τ</b>.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</SimLayout>

<script>
  import { setupCanvas, makeText, subHtml } from '../../../lib/sim';

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Drude, taken literally. An electron is accelerated by the field for a while,
  // then a collision randomises its velocity completely. The mean time between
  // collisions is τ, so the average velocity is a·τ = (eE/m)τ, and putting that
  // into I = n e A v_d gives V = IR with R = ρL/A and ρ = m/(n e² τ).
  //
  // The particles on screen run that story in scaled units; the readout runs it
  // in SI for the metal that is selected. Temperature enters in exactly one
  // place, by shortening τ, which is enough to make a light bulb behave like a
  // light bulb.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  const E_CHARGE = 1.602e-19;

  interface Material {
    name: string;
    rho20: number; // Ω·m at 20 °C
    alpha: number; // per K
    n: number; // carriers per m³
    melt: number; // °C
    hot: string; // colour it glows
  }

  const MATERIALS: Record<string, Material> = {
    copper: { name: 'Copper', rho20: 1.68e-8, alpha: 0.00393, n: 8.5e28, melt: 1085, hot: '#ffb347' },
    tungsten: { name: 'Tungsten', rho20: 5.6e-8, alpha: 0.0045, n: 6.3e28, melt: 3422, hot: '#fff1c9' },
    nichrome: { name: 'Nichrome', rho20: 1.1e-6, alpha: 0.0004, n: 6.0e28, melt: 1400, hot: '#ff9b57' },
    carbon: { name: 'Carbon', rho20: 3.5e-5, alpha: -0.0005, n: 1.0e25, melt: 3600, hot: '#ff7b4a' },
  };

  const state = {
    mat: 'nichrome',
    plot: 'scope' as 'scope' | 'iv' | 'power',
    V: 1, // volts
    L: 500, // mm
    A: 0.1, // mm²
    tamb: 20, // °C
    thermal: true,
    heat: false,
    trail: true,
    rate: 1,
    running: true,
  };

  let T = 20; // the sample's own temperature, °C

  const mat = () => MATERIALS[state.mat];
  const rho = () => mat().rho20 * (1 + mat().alpha * (T - 20));
  const Rof = () => Math.max(1e-9, (rho() * (state.L * 1e-3)) / (state.A * 1e-6));
  const current = () => state.V / Rof();
  const power = () => state.V * current();
  const field = () => state.V / (state.L * 1e-3); // V/m
  /** Drift speed from the current, which is the same thing as aτ. */
  const vdrift = () => current() / (mat().n * E_CHARGE * state.A * 1e-6);
  /** Thermal speed, room temperature order of magnitude. */
  const vthermal = () => 1.1e5 * Math.sqrt((T + 273) / 293);
  /** τ read back out of the resistivity, since ρ = m/(n e² τ). */
  const tau = () => 9.11e-31 / (mat().n * E_CHARGE * E_CHARGE * rho());

  // --- thermal balance -------------------------------------------------------

  const SIGMA = 5.67e-8;
  function thermalStep(dt: number) {
    if (!state.heat) {
      T += (state.tamb - T) * Math.min(1, dt * 6);
      return;
    }
    const vol = state.L * 1e-3 * state.A * 1e-6; // m³
    const surf = 4 * Math.sqrt(state.A * 1e-6) * state.L * 1e-3; // m²
    const c = 3.5e6; // J/(m³·K), near enough for a metal
    const Tk = T + 273, Ak = state.tamb + 273;
    const lost = 25 * surf * (T - state.tamb) + 0.4 * SIGMA * surf * (Tk ** 4 - Ak ** 4);
    const dT = ((power() - lost) / (c * vol)) * dt;
    T += Math.max(-4000 * dt, Math.min(4000 * dt, dT));
    T = Math.max(-273, T);
  }

  // --- the particle picture --------------------------------------------------

  const SLAB = { x: 20, y: 74, w: 960, h: 330 };
  let box = { x: 0, y: 0, w: 0, h: 0 };

  type Electron = { x: number; y: number; vx: number; vy: number };
  let elec: Electron[] = [];
  let trail: { x: number; y: number }[] = [];
  let flashes: { x: number; y: number; life: number }[] = [];
  let vbar = 0; // measured mean vx, px/s
  let vhist: number[] = [];

  const rnd = (() => {
    let s = 424242;
    return () => ((s = (s * 1664525 + 1013904223) >>> 0) / 4294967296);
  })();

  function geometry() {
    // The slab is drawn to scale: longer samples stretch, fatter ones thicken,
    // so R = ρL/A is a shape you can see and not only a formula.
    const w = 240 + (state.L / 2000) * 560;
    const h = 60 + Math.sqrt(state.A / 2) * 140;
    box = { x: SLAB.x + (SLAB.w - w) / 2, y: SLAB.y + (SLAB.h - h) / 2 + 10, w, h };
  }

  function seed() {
    geometry();
    elec = [];
    for (let k = 0; k < 190; k++)
      elec.push({
        x: box.x + rnd() * box.w,
        y: box.y + rnd() * box.h,
        vx: 0,
        vy: 0,
      });
    trail = [];
    flashes = [];
    vhist = [];
    vbar = 0;
    T = state.tamb;
  }

  const VTH = 150; // px/s, the scaled thermal speed
  const TAU0 = 0.075; // s, the scaled mean free time at 20 °C

  function scatter(e: Electron) {
    const a = rnd() * Math.PI * 2;
    const s = state.thermal ? VTH * (0.5 + rnd()) * Math.sqrt((T + 273) / 293) : 0;
    e.vx = Math.cos(a) * s;
    e.vy = Math.sin(a) * s;
  }

  function particles(dt: number) {
    // scaled acceleration: proportional to the real field, capped so the dots
    // stay watchable at six volts across a few millimetres
    const accel = Math.min(2600, (field() / 40) * 1.4);
    const tauS = TAU0 / Math.max(0.15, 1 + mat().alpha * (T - 20));
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < elec.length; i++) {
      const e = elec[i];
      e.vx += accel * dt;
      e.x += e.vx * dt;
      e.y += e.vy * dt;
      if (e.y < box.y) { e.y = box.y; e.vy = Math.abs(e.vy); }
      if (e.y > box.y + box.h) { e.y = box.y + box.h; e.vy = -Math.abs(e.vy); }
      if (e.x > box.x + box.w) { e.x = box.x; if (i === 0) trail = []; }
      if (e.x < box.x) { e.x = box.x + box.w; if (i === 0) trail = []; }
      if (rnd() < dt / tauS) {
        if (flashes.length < 40) flashes.push({ x: e.x, y: e.y, life: 1 });
        scatter(e);
      }
      sum += e.vx;
    }
    vbar += (sum / elec.length - vbar) * Math.min(1, dt * 3);
    vhist.push(vbar);
    if (vhist.length > 460) vhist.shift();
    if (state.trail) {
      trail.push({ x: elec[0].x, y: elec[0].y });
      if (trail.length > 320) trail.shift();
    }
    for (const f of flashes) f.life -= dt * 2.4;
    flashes = flashes.filter((f) => f.life > 0);
  }

  // --- canvas ----------------------------------------------------------------

  const canvas = document.getElementById('res') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const { ctx, W, H } = setupCanvas(canvas, () => draw());
  const cssv = (n: string) => getComputedStyle(canvas).getPropertyValue(n).trim();
  const inkA = (a: number) => `rgba(${cssv('--stage-ink') || '232,233,236'},${a})`;
  const { label, textWidth } = makeText(ctx, inkA);

  const ACC = '#7c8cff'; // the field
  const TEAL = '#5eead4'; // electrons
  const WARM = '#f59e0b'; // heat
  const GREEN = '#34d399'; // current
  const HOT = '#f9736a';

  // One plot at a time under the scene; the side panel picks which.
  const PLOT = { x: 20, y: 448, w: 960, h: 230 };
  const IV = PLOT, SCOPE = PLOT, PWR = PLOT;

  function rr(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, r: number) {
    const rad = Math.min(r, Math.abs(w) / 2, Math.abs(h) / 2);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x + rad, y);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w - rad, y);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x + w, y, x + w, y + rad);
    ctx.lineTo(x + w, y + h - rad);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x + w, y + h, x + w - rad, y + h);
    ctx.lineTo(x + rad, y + h);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x, y + h, x, y + h - rad);
    ctx.lineTo(x, y + rad);
    ctx.quadraticCurveTo(x, y, x + rad, y);
    ctx.closePath();
  }
  function panel(p: { x: number; y: number; w: number; h: number }, title: string) {
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.1);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    rr(p.x, p.y, p.w, p.h, 10);
    ctx.stroke();
    label(title, p.x + 2, p.y - 9, 0.5, 'left', 11);
  }
  function arrow(x1: number, y1: number, x2: number, y2: number, size = 6) {
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x1, y1);
    ctx.lineTo(x2, y2);
    ctx.stroke();
    const a = Math.atan2(y2 - y1, x2 - x1);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x2, y2);
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - size * Math.cos(a - 0.4), y2 - size * Math.sin(a - 0.4));
    ctx.lineTo(x2 - size * Math.cos(a + 0.4), y2 - size * Math.sin(a + 0.4));
    ctx.closePath();
    ctx.fill();
  }

  function eng(v: number, unit: string, digits = 2): string {
    const a = Math.abs(v);
    if (a === 0) return `0 ${unit}`;
    const steps: [number, string][] = [
      [1e-15, 'f'], [1e-12, 'p'], [1e-9, 'n'], [1e-6, 'µ'], [1e-3, 'm'],
      [1, ''], [1e3, 'k'], [1e6, 'M'], [1e9, 'G'],
    ];
    let pick = steps[0];
    for (const s of steps) if (a >= s[0]) pick = s;
    return `${(v / pick[0]).toFixed(digits)} ${pick[1]}${unit}`;
  }

  /** How hot metal looks, roughly: dull red, orange, then white. */
  function glow(t: number) {
    const f = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (t - 500) / 1600));
    if (f <= 0) return null;
    const r = 255;
    const g = Math.round(60 + 180 * f);
    const b = Math.round(20 + 190 * f * f);
    return { css: `rgb(${r},${g},${b})`, a: 0.12 + 0.55 * f };
  }

  // --- the slab --------------------------------------------------------------

  function drawSlab() {
    panel(SLAB, 'INSIDE THE MATERIAL');
    geometry();

    // terminals
    ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.28);
    rr(box.x - 44, box.y - 12, 44, box.h + 24, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    rr(box.x + box.w, box.y - 12, 44, box.h + 24, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    label('+', box.x - 22, box.y + box.h / 2 + 6, 0.8, 'center', 17, WARM);
    label('−', box.x + box.w + 22, box.y + box.h / 2 + 6, 0.8, 'center', 17, ACC);

    // the sample itself, glowing if it is hot enough to
    const g = glow(T);
    ctx.fillStyle = g ? g.css : inkA(0.04);
    ctx.globalAlpha = g ? g.a : 1;
    ctx.fillRect(box.x, box.y, box.w, box.h);
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.25);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.strokeRect(box.x, box.y, box.w, box.h);

    // the lattice, shaking harder the hotter it is
    const jitter = Math.min(2.4, 0.25 + Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, T + 273) / 293) * 0.55);
    const step = 44;
    const t = performance.now() / 220;
    ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.34);
    for (let x = box.x + step / 2; x < box.x + box.w; x += step)
      for (let y = box.y + step / 2; y < box.y + box.h; y += step) {
        const dx = Math.sin(t + x * 0.7 + y * 0.3) * jitter;
        const dy = Math.cos(t * 1.13 + x * 0.4 - y * 0.6) * jitter;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(x + dx, y + dy, 3.4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }

    // the field that does the pushing
    ctx.strokeStyle = ACC;
    ctx.fillStyle = ACC;
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
    const fy = box.y + box.h + 30;
    for (let k = 0; k < 5; k++) {
      const x0 = box.x + 24 + (k * (box.w - 48)) / 4;
      arrow(x0 - 16, fy, x0 + 16, fy, 5);
    }
    label(
      `E = V / L = ${eng(field(), 'V/m', 1)}`,
      box.x + box.w / 2,
      fy + 20,
      0.75,
      'center',
      11.5,
      ACC
    );

    // the collisions, where the energy is handed over
    for (const f of flashes) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(245,158,11,${(f.life * 0.5).toFixed(3)})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(f.x, f.y, 4 + (1 - f.life) * 9, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    // one electron's path, so the zigzag is not just an assertion
    if (state.trail && trail.length > 2) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(94,234,212,0.55)';
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
      ctx.beginPath();
      trail.forEach((p, k) => (k ? ctx.lineTo(p.x, p.y) : ctx.moveTo(p.x, p.y)));
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    for (let i = 0; i < elec.length; i++) {
      const e = elec[i];
      ctx.fillStyle = i === 0 && state.trail ? '#ffffff' : TEAL;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(e.x, e.y, i === 0 && state.trail ? 4.4 : 3, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }

    // the geometry, named
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.3);
    ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.3);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    const ly = box.y - 22;
    arrow(box.x, ly, box.x + box.w, ly, 5);
    arrow(box.x + box.w, ly, box.x, ly, 5);
    label(
      state.L >= 1000 ? `L = ${(state.L / 1000).toFixed(2)} m` : `L = ${state.L} mm`,
      box.x + box.w / 2,
      ly - 8,
      0.55,
      'center',
      11
    );
    label(`A = ${state.A.toFixed(2)} mm²`, box.x - 52, box.y + box.h / 2 + 4, 0.55, 'right', 11);

    label(
      `R = ρL/A = ${fmtR(Rof())}`,
      SLAB.x + SLAB.w - 12,
      SLAB.y + SLAB.h - 14,
      0.7,
      'right',
      12,
      GREEN
    );

    const legend: [string, string][] = [
      [TEAL, 'electron'],
      [inkA(0.5), 'lattice ion'],
      [WARM, 'collision, heat handed over'],
    ];
    let lx = SLAB.x + SLAB.w - 8;
    for (let k = legend.length - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
      const [c, tx] = legend[k];
      const tw = textWidth(tx, 10.5);
      label(tx, lx, SLAB.y + 20, 0.5, 'right', 10.5);
      ctx.fillStyle = c;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(lx - tw - 10, SLAB.y + 16, 4, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
      lx -= tw + 28;
    }
  }

  // --- I against V -----------------------------------------------------------

  // The bent curve of a self-heating sample is only visible if we remember
  // where it has been, so keep the points it has actually sat at.
  let ivTrace: { v: number; i: number }[] = [];

  function drawIV() {
    panel(IV, 'CURRENT AGAINST VOLTAGE');
    const px = IV.x + 46, py = IV.y + 18, pw = IV.w - 62, ph = IV.h - 56;
    const vmax = 6;
    let imax = Math.max(current(), ...ivTrace.map((p) => p.i), 1e-9) * 1.15;
    const X = (v: number) => px + (v / vmax) * pw;
    const Y = (i: number) => py + ph - (i / imax) * ph;

    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.18);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();
    label('V', px + pw, py + ph + 16, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label('I', px - 8, py + 8, 0.35, 'right', 10);
    label(eng(imax, 'A', 1), px - 8, py + 20, 0.3, 'right', 9);

    // the straight line the sample would follow if R never moved
    const R0 = (mat().rho20 * (1 + mat().alpha * (state.tamb - 20)) * state.L * 1e-3) / (state.A * 1e-6);
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.28);
    ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(X(0), Y(0));
    ctx.lineTo(X(vmax), Y(vmax / R0));
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    label('ohmic, cold', px + pw - 4, py + 14, 0.4, 'right', 10);

    if (ivTrace.length > 1) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = GREEN;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ivTrace.forEach((p, k) => (k ? ctx.lineTo(X(p.v), Y(p.i)) : ctx.moveTo(X(p.v), Y(p.i))));
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    ctx.fillStyle = GREEN;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(X(state.V), Y(current()), 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    label(
      state.heat ? 'it bends because R follows the heat' : 'a straight line, and that is Ohm',
      px - 30,
      py + ph + 32,
      0.4,
      'left',
      10
    );
  }

  // --- the average velocity --------------------------------------------------

  function drawScope() {
    panel(SCOPE, 'AVERAGE VELOCITY OF ALL THE ELECTRONS');
    const px = SCOPE.x + 42, py = SCOPE.y + 20, pw = SCOPE.w - 56, ph = SCOPE.h - 66;
    const accel = Math.min(2600, (field() / 40) * 1.4);
    const tauS = TAU0 / Math.max(0.15, 1 + mat().alpha * (T - 20));
    const pred = accel * tauS;
    const scale = Math.max(pred * 1.9, ...vhist.map((v) => Math.abs(v) * 1.25), 12);
    const Y = (v: number) => py + ph - (v / scale) * ph;

    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.18);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();

    ctx.strokeStyle = ACC;
    ctx.setLineDash([5, 4]);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.5;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, Y(pred));
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, Y(pred));
    ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
    label('a τ', px + pw + 2, Y(pred) + 4, 0.9, 'right', 11, ACC);

    if (vhist.length > 1) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = TEAL;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      vhist.forEach((v, k) =>
        k
          ? ctx.lineTo(px + (k / (vhist.length - 1)) * pw, Y(v))
          : ctx.moveTo(px, Y(v))
      );
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    label('measured', px + 6, py + 12, 0.8, 'left', 10.5, TEAL);
    label('predicted', px + 76, py + 12, 0.8, 'left', 10.5, ACC);
    label('the field accelerates, the lattice resets,', px - 26, py + ph + 22, 0.4, 'left', 10);
    label('and the average of that is the current', px - 26, py + ph + 38, 0.4, 'left', 10);
  }

  // --- power and temperature -------------------------------------------------

  function drawPower() {
    panel(PWR, 'WHERE THE ENERGY GOES');
    const px = PWR.x + 22, py = PWR.y + 24;
    label('P = V I = I²R', px, py + 6, 0.9, 'left', 14, WARM);
    label(eng(power(), 'W'), px + PWR.w - 44, py + 6, 1, 'right', 15, WARM);

    // a temperature column, with the melting point drawn as a wall
    const bx = px, by = py + 32, bw = PWR.w - 60, bh = 20;
    const tmax = Math.max(mat().melt * 1.15, 400);
    ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.07);
    rr(bx, by, bw, bh, 5);
    ctx.fill();
    const f = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (T + 50) / (tmax + 50)));
    const g = glow(T);
    ctx.fillStyle = g ? g.css : WARM;
    rr(bx, by, Math.max(3, f * bw), bh, 5);
    ctx.fill();
    const mx = bx + ((mat().melt + 50) / (tmax + 50)) * bw;
    ctx.strokeStyle = HOT;
    ctx.lineWidth = 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(mx, by - 5);
    ctx.lineTo(mx, by + bh + 5);
    ctx.stroke();
    label('melts', mx + 5, by + bh + 18, 0.6, 'left', 10, HOT);
    label(`${T.toFixed(0)} °C`, bx, by + bh + 18, 0.85, 'left', 12, WARM);

    // the balance that decides where it settles
    const yy = by + 54;
    label(
      state.heat ? 'in, from the field' : 'held at ambient',
      px,
      yy,
      0.5,
      'left',
      11
    );
    label(state.heat ? eng(power(), 'W') : '—', px + PWR.w - 44, yy, 0.8, 'right', 11, GREEN);
    label('out, to the air and as light', px, yy + 20, 0.5, 'left', 11);
    const surf = 4 * Math.sqrt(state.A * 1e-6) * state.L * 1e-3;
    const lost =
      25 * surf * (T - state.tamb) +
      0.4 * SIGMA * surf * ((T + 273) ** 4 - (state.tamb + 273) ** 4);
    label(state.heat ? eng(lost, 'W') : '—', px + PWR.w - 44, yy + 20, 0.8, 'right', 11, ACC);
    label(
      T > mat().melt
        ? 'this sample no longer exists'
        : state.heat
          ? 'it settles where the two balance'
          : 'switch self-heating on to watch it fight',
      px,
      yy + 46,
      0.45,
      'left',
      10.5,
      T > mat().melt ? HOT : undefined
    );
    label(`resistivity now ${eng(rho(), 'Ω·m', 2)}`, px, yy + 66, 0.45, 'left', 10.5);
  }

  const fmtR = (r: number) =>
    r >= 1e6 ? (r / 1e6).toFixed(2) + ' MΩ'
    : r >= 1e3 ? (r / 1e3).toFixed(2) + ' kΩ'
    : r >= 1 ? r.toFixed(2) + ' Ω'
    : r >= 1e-3 ? (r * 1e3).toFixed(2) + ' mΩ'
    : (r * 1e6).toFixed(1) + ' µΩ';

  // --- readouts --------------------------------------------------------------

  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;
  const setRow = (n: number, lab: string, val: string) => {
    el('l' + n).innerHTML = subHtml(lab);
    el('r' + n).textContent = val;
  };
  function updateReadout() {
    setRow(1, 'Resistance R', fmtR(Rof()));
    setRow(2, 'Current I', eng(current(), 'A'));
    setRow(3, 'Power P', eng(power(), 'W'));
    setRow(4, 'Resistivity ρ', eng(rho(), 'Ω·m'));
    setRow(5, 'Mean free time τ', eng(tau(), 's'));
    setRow(6, 'Drift speed v_d', eng(vdrift(), 'm/s'));
    setRow(7, 'Thermal speed', eng(vthermal(), 'm/s', 1));
    setRow(
      8,
      'Thermal / drift',
      vdrift() > 0 ? (vthermal() / vdrift()).toExponential(1) + '×' : '—'
    );
  }

  // --- frame -----------------------------------------------------------------

  function draw() {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, W, H);
    label(`Resistor · ${mat().name}`, 20, 30, 0.85, 'left', 14);
    drawSlab();
    if (state.plot === 'iv') drawIV();
    else if (state.plot === 'power') drawPower();
    else drawScope();
  }

  let last = performance.now();
  function frame(now: number) {
    let dt = (now - last) / 1000;
    last = now;
    dt = Math.min(0.05, Math.max(0, dt)) * state.rate;
    if (state.running) {
      particles(dt);
      thermalStep(dt);
      const i = current();
      const prev = ivTrace[ivTrace.length - 1];
      if (!prev || Math.abs(prev.v - state.V) > 1e-3 || Math.abs(prev.i - i) / (i || 1) > 5e-3) {
        ivTrace.push({ v: state.V, i });
        if (ivTrace.length > 400) ivTrace.shift();
      }
    }
    draw();
    updateReadout();
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }

  // --- controls --------------------------------------------------------------

  const runBtn = el('run');
  runBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    state.running = !state.running;
    runBtn.textContent = state.running ? 'Pause' : 'Run';
  });
  el('reset').addEventListener('click', () => {
    ivTrace = [];
    seed();
  });

  function bindRange(id: string, apply: (n: number) => void) {
    const input = document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;
    const run = () => apply(+input.value);
    input.addEventListener('input', run);
    run();
  }
  bindRange('v', (n) => {
    state.V = n / 100;
    el('vval').textContent = `${state.V.toFixed(2)} V`;
  });
  bindRange('len', (n) => {
    state.L = n * 10;
    el('lval').textContent = state.L >= 1000 ? `${(state.L / 1000).toFixed(2)} m` : `${state.L} mm`;
    geometry();
  });
  bindRange('area', (n) => {
    state.A = n / 100;
    el('aval').textContent = `${state.A.toFixed(2)} mm²`;
    geometry();
  });
  bindRange('tamb', (n) => {
    state.tamb = n;
    el('tval').textContent = `${n} °C`;
  });
  bindRange('rate', (n) => {
    state.rate = n / 100;
    el('rateval').textContent = `${state.rate.toFixed(1)}×`;
  });

  for (const id of ['thermal', 'heat', 'trail'] as const) {
    const input = document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;
    input.addEventListener('change', () => {
      (state as Record<string, unknown>)[id] = input.checked;
      if (id === 'trail') trail = [];
      if (id === 'heat') ivTrace = [];
    });
  }

  el('plot').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
    if (!btn) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('#plot button').forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
    btn.classList.add('active');
    state.plot = btn.dataset.p as 'scope' | 'iv' | 'power';
  });

  el('mat').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
    if (!btn) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('#mat button').forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
    btn.classList.add('active');
    state.mat = btn.dataset.m!;
    ivTrace = [];
    seed();
  });

  seed();
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
</script>

<style>
  #res {
    touch-action: none;
  }
  .check {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .check input {
    accent-color: var(--accent);
    width: 15px;
    height: 15px;
  }
  .scale-hint {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    margin-top: 3px;
  }
</style>
